E+=[
X('yeha-temple','Great Temple of Yeha (Dʿmt)',H,'confirmed',2,-700,['archaeological','epigraphic'],
 "Yeha in Tigray, northern Ethiopia, preserves the Great Temple, a tall ashlar stone sanctuary usually dated to around the 7th century BCE, together with the nearby Grat Beal Gebri palace complex. Inscriptions and architecture show close links with the Sabaean culture of South Arabia, and Yeha is regarded as a centre of the pre-Aksumite kingdom of Dʿmt. It is often described as one of the oldest standing buildings in sub-Saharan Africa.",
 [('Colony or local kingdom','Older scholarship treated Dʿmt as a South Arabian colony; more recent work stresses a mixed Ethio-Sabaean elite drawing on strong local traditions.'),
  ('Lost Solomonic city','Ethiopian tradition and popular writing sometimes link Yeha to the Queen of Sheba and Solomon; this is legend layered on a real pre-Aksumite site, not an archaeological finding.')],
 ['wiki:Yeha','wiki:Dʿmt'],img='Yeha',dateNote='Great Temple c. 7th century BCE (dates debated)'),
X('fasil-ghebbi','Fasil Ghebbi, Gondar',H,'confirmed',2,1636,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Fasil Ghebbi is the walled royal enclosure at Gondar, Ethiopia, begun by Emperor Fasilides (Fasil) after he made Gondar his capital around 1636. Successive emperors added castles, banqueting halls, churches and Fasilides' Bath, blending Ethiopian, Indian, Arab and Portuguese-influenced styles. Gondar remained the imperial capital until the mid-19th century, and the site was listed by UNESCO in 1979.",
 [('Foreign builders','Some accounts credit Portuguese or Indian masons for the castles; historians see foreign influence absorbed into a distinctly Ethiopian royal architecture.'),
  ('Folk legends of the founding','Local stories say the site was chosen by prophecy or by following a buffalo to a lake; these are founding legends rather than documentary history.')],
 ['wiki:Fasil_Ghebbi',f'url:{U}19/|UNESCO: Fasil Ghebbi, Gondar Region|heritage'],img='Fasil_Ghebbi',end=1855),
X('tiya-stelae','Tiya stelae',H,'confirmed',3,1200,['archaeological'],
 "Tiya, in the Gurage zone of southern Ethiopia, is one of nine megalithic pillar sites in the Gurage Zone. Its stone pillars, 32 of them engraved with enigmatic symbols including swords, mark a burial complex and were listed by UNESCO in 1980. Excavations led by Roger Joussaume dated the site to roughly the 11th–13th centuries CE, with later estimates spanning the 10th–15th centuries; who built them is not recorded.",
 [('Warrior tally markers','The carved swords are often read as indicating warriors or the number of enemies killed; this remains an interpretation.'),
  ('"Gran\'s stones"','Local people call such stelae Yegragn Dingay, "Gran\'s stone", crediting the 16th-century conqueror Ahmad Gragn; the monuments are centuries older than him, so this is a later folk attribution.'),
  ('Older lost culture','Because the builders are unnamed in written records, some popular accounts assign the stelae to a vanished prehistoric people; excavations point to medieval burials, within a much older Ethiopian megalith tradition.')],
 ['wiki:Tiya_(archaeological_site)',f'url:{U}12/|UNESCO: Tiya|heritage'],img='Tiya_(archaeological_site)',dateNote='c. 10th–15th centuries CE (Joussaume: 11th–13th)'),
X('makuria-faras','Makuria and the Faras Cathedral frescoes',H,'confirmed',2,570,['archaeological','textual','art historical'],
 "Makuria was a Christian Nubian kingdom on the middle Nile, with its capital at Old Dongola, from roughly the 6th century until about the 15th. After repelling Arab armies in 642 and 652 it concluded the Baqt treaty with Egypt, a trade-and-peace arrangement that lasted for centuries. When Lake Nasser was about to flood Lower Nubia, a Polish mission led by Kazimierz Michałowski (1961–64) uncovered over a hundred wall paintings in the cathedral at Faras, which are now in Warsaw and Khartoum.",
 [('A forgotten African Christendom','Historians emphasise how long Nubian Christianity endured and how little it features in general histories; the extent of its contacts with Byzantium and Ethiopia is still researched.'),
  ('Saved from the flood','The Faras rescue is sometimes told as a near-miraculous recovery; it was part of the large UNESCO Nubia campaign, and much of the site itself now lies under the lake.')],
 ['wiki:Makuria','wiki:Faras'],img='Faras',end=1500,dateNote='c. 6th century to c. 15th century CE'),
X('queen-gudit','Queen Gudit (Yodit)',M,'disputed',2,960,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "Ethiopian tradition remembers Gudit (Yodit, 'Judith') as a queen who sacked Aksum and destroyed churches in the 10th century, bringing about the end of the Aksumite kingdom. The 10th-century geographer Ibn Hawqal mentions, without naming her, a queen who had seized power and devastated the country around that time. Her identity is unclear; one proposal identifies her with Masoba Warq, daughter of the last Aksumite king Dil Na'od. Her religion and even her existence as one person are debated, and the traditions about her appear to have taken centuries to reach their final form.",
 [('Jewish or pagan queen','Some traditions make her Jewish (linked with the Beta Israel); others identify her as a non-Christian ruler from the southern kingdom of Damot or from Agaw lands.'),
  ('Legend built on a real crisis','Many historians accept a real 10th-century invasion led by a queen while treating the detailed legends about her motives and curses as later elaboration.')],
 ['wiki:Gudit'],img='Gudit',dateNote='10th century CE (traditional)'),
X('famadihana','Famadihana (turning of the bones)',H,'confirmed',3,1650,['ethnographic','oral tradition'],
 "Famadihana is a funerary tradition of the Malagasy highlands, especially among the Merina and Betsileo, in which families open the ancestral tomb, rewrap the remains in fresh silk shrouds, and dance with them before returning them. It is usually held every five to seven years and is understood as honouring the ancestors (razana), whose spirits are believed to fully join the ancestral world only after the body's complete decomposition and proper ceremonies. Early missionaries discouraged it; the Catholic Church now regards it as cultural, while many Evangelical Christians have abandoned it. The Malagasy government forbids the rite for people who died of plague.",
 [('Ancient or recent custom','It may have taken its present form only around the 17th century, possibly adapting older double-funeral customs from Southeast Asia and Oceania brought by Madagascar\'s Austronesian settlers.'),
  ('Talking with the dead','Participants describe the ritual as sustaining a living relationship with ancestors; this is a religious understanding, not an empirical claim.')],
 ['wiki:Famadihana'],img='Famadihana',dateNote='Present form perhaps since c. 17th century; origins debated'),
X('ambohimanga','Royal Hill of Ambohimanga',H,'confirmed',3,1787,['archaeological','oral tradition','textual'],
 "Ambohimanga, a fortified hilltop village north of Antananarivo, was the spiritual and political seat of the Merina kings. From here Andrianampoinimerina (r. c. 1787–1810) began unifying the Imerina highlands, a process his son Radama I extended over much of Madagascar. The hill is still a pilgrimage site for ancestor veneration and was listed by UNESCO in 2001.",
 [('Sacred city','Merina tradition treats the hill as sacred and once forbade certain foods and outsiders; heritage bodies recognise it as a living religious landscape.'),
  ('Fixed founding date','Oral royal genealogies give earlier origins for the settlement; the documented political rise belongs to the late 18th century.')],
 ['wiki:Ambohimanga',f'url:{U}950/|UNESCO: Royal Hill of Ambohimanga|heritage'],img='Ambohimanga'),
X('hagia-sophia','Hagia Sophia',H,'confirmed',1,537,['archaeological','textual','architectural'],
 "Hagia Sophia in Istanbul was built for Emperor Justinian I between 532 and 537 by Anthemius of Tralles and Isidore of Miletus, after the previous church burned in the Nika riots. Its vast dome partly collapsed in 558 and was rebuilt higher. It served as the cathedral of Constantinople for over 900 years, became a mosque after 1453, a museum in 1934, and a mosque again in 2020.",
 [('A priest inside the wall','A Greek legend says a priest celebrating the liturgy as the Ottomans broke in vanished into a wall and will reappear when the city is Christian again; it is a folk legend of the conquest.'),
  ('Sacred geometry and acoustics','Modern studies explore the acoustics and geometry of the dome; claims of encoded esoteric knowledge go beyond the architectural evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Hagia_Sophia',f'url:{U}356/|UNESCO: Historic Areas of Istanbul|heritage'],img='Hagia_Sophia'),
X('fall-of-constantinople','Fall of Constantinople (1453)',H,'confirmed',1,1453,['textual','archaeological'],
 "On 29 May 1453 the Ottoman army of Sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople after a siege of about 53 days, ending the Byzantine Empire; the last emperor, Constantine XI Palaiologos, died in the fighting. The Ottomans used large bombards, including guns cast by the engineer Orban, and dragged ships overland into the Golden Horn to bypass its defensive chain. The city became the Ottoman capital.",
 [('The Kerkoporta gate','Doukas reports that a small postern gate left open let the attackers in; some historians doubt the story or think it was minor.'),
  ('The sleeping emperor','Greek legend says Constantine XI was turned to marble by an angel and will awaken to retake the city, a sleeping-hero motif shared with many cultures.')],
 ['wiki:Fall_of_Constantinople'],img='Fall_of_Constantinople'),
X('nika-riots','Nika riots (532)',H,'confirmed',2,532,['textual'],
 "In January 532 the Blue and Green chariot-racing factions of Constantinople united against Emperor Justinian I, shouting 'Nika!' ('Conquer!'), burning much of the city including the church of Hagia Sophia, and proclaiming Hypatius emperor. According to Procopius, Empress Theodora persuaded Justinian not to flee. The generals Belisarius and Mundus trapped the crowd in the Hippodrome, and Procopius reports around 30,000 deaths.",
 [('Theodora\'s speech','Her famous line that \"royalty is a good burial shroud\" comes from Procopius, who may have shaped it rhetorically.'),
  ('Size of the massacre','The figure of 30,000 comes from ancient authors and cannot be checked independently; it may be exaggerated.')],
 ['wiki:Nika_riots'],img='Nika_riots'),
]
# --- part 2: Mongolia / Siberia / Arctic / Mesopotamia / Anatolia
E+=[
X('karakorum','Karakorum, Mongol capital',H,'confirmed',2,1220,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Karakorum, in the Orkhon Valley of Mongolia, was founded as a base by Genghis Khan around 1220 and walled as the imperial capital under Ögedei in 1235. The Franciscan William of Rubruck, who visited in 1254, described a quarter for Muslims and Chinese, many temples and a silver tree fountain made by the captive Parisian goldsmith Guillaume Boucher, an automaton in which a trumpeting angel signalled serpents to pour out drinks. Kublai moved the capital to Dadu (Beijing); Ming armies damaged the city in 1372 and razed it in 1380, and in 1585 the Erdene Zuu monastery was built beside it, largely from Karakorum's stones and bricks.",
 [('Silver tree fountain','Rubruck\'s description is the only detailed account; its mechanics are reconstructed from his text.'),
  ('Cosmopolitan capital','Excavations confirm workshops, a palace area and mixed communities, supporting Rubruck while correcting some of his details.')],
 ['wiki:Karakorum',f'url:{U}1081/|UNESCO: Orkhon Valley Cultural Landscape|heritage'],img='Karakorum',end=1585),
X('secret-history-mongols','Secret History of the Mongols',H,'confirmed',2,1228,['textual'],
 "The Secret History of the Mongols is the oldest surviving literary work in Mongolian, written for the royal family after Genghis Khan's death (often dated to 1228 or 1240). It traces his ancestry to a blue-grey wolf and a fallow doe and tells how the widow Alan Gua conceived sons by a shining yellow man who came in through the smoke-hole of her tent. It then narrates Temüjin's rise to become Genghis Khan. It survives mainly in a Chinese-character transcription made in the Ming period.",
 [('History or epic','Historians use it as a key source on Genghis Khan\'s life while recognising its epic, partisan and legendary layers.'),
  ('Light-being ancestor','Ancient-astronaut writers have read the shining visitor of Alan Gua as an extraterrestrial; scholars see a sky-born royal origin myth common to Inner Asian dynasties.')],
 ['wiki:The_Secret_History_of_the_Mongols'],img='The_Secret_History_of_the_Mongols',dateNote='Composed after 1227; date debated (1228/1240/1252)'),
X('malta-boy','Mal\'ta boy (MA-1)',H,'confirmed',2,-22000,['genetic','archaeological'],
 "The Mal'ta boy is the skeleton of a child buried about 24,000 years ago at Mal'ta near Lake Baikal in Siberia, a site also known for ivory figurines of women and birds. His genome, published in 2013, showed that he belonged to a population related to western Eurasians and that this population contributed an estimated 14–38% of the ancestry of Native Americans. The result reshaped models of how the Americas were peopled.",
 [('Western Eurasians in the Americas','Early fringe readings claimed the genome proved Europeans reached America first; the data show deep shared ancestry in Siberia, not a European migration.'),
  ('Ancient North Eurasians','Geneticists now call this lineage \"Ancient North Eurasian\" and also find it in later Europeans, linking peoples on both sides of the world.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature12736','wiki:Mal\'ta–Buret\'_culture'],img='Mal\'ta–Buret\'_culture',dateNote='c. 24,000 years ago'),
X('orkhon-inscriptions','Orkhon inscriptions',H,'confirmed',2,732,['epigraphic','textual'],
 "The Orkhon inscriptions are memorial stelae erected in the Orkhon Valley of Mongolia in 732 and 735 for the Second Turkic Khaganate prince Kül Tigin and his brother Bilge Khagan. Written in the Old Turkic runiform script (with a Chinese text on one), they are the oldest known extended texts in a Turkic language. Found by Nikolai Yadrintsev's expedition in 1889, the script was deciphered by the Danish linguist Vilhelm Thomsen in 1893.",
 [('Origins of the script','Scholars debate whether the Old Turkic letters derive from Sogdian/Aramaic models, tamga clan signs, or both.'),
  ('Runes of a lost world-empire','Because the script resembles Germanic runes, some fringe writers have linked the two; linguists treat the resemblance as superficial.')],
 ['wiki:Orkhon_inscriptions'],img='Orkhon_inscriptions',dateNote='732 and 735 CE'),
X('patomskiy-crater','Patomskiy crater',H,'disputed',3,1700,['geological'],
 "The Patomskiy crater is a cone of broken limestone about 40 m high and 160 m across with a central mound, in the forest of Irkutsk Oblast, Siberia. It was reported in 1949 by the geologist Vadim Kolpakov, and tree-ring studies suggest it formed only about 300 years ago. Local Yakut people called it the 'fire eagle's nest'. Its origin is still debated.",
 [('Deep-fluid eruption','A study by V. S. Antipin and A. M. Fedorov argued from its shape, structure and chemistry that it is volcanic in origin, formed by a rapid outburst of deep fluids and gases (CO2, water, CO, hydrogen).'),
  ('Meteorite or Tunguska fragment','Early theories proposed an impact, including a fragment linked to Tunguska; the lack of meteoritic material makes this unlikely.'),
  ('Alien or secret-weapon site','Popular media have suggested alien activity or Soviet secrets; no evidence supports these ideas.')],
 ['wiki:Patomskiy_crater'],img='Patomskiy_crater',dateNote='Formation estimated at c. 300 years ago (dendrochronology)'),
X('andree-balloon','Andrée\'s Arctic balloon expedition',H,'confirmed',2,1897,['textual','photographic','archaeological'],
 "On 11 July 1897 Salomon August Andrée, Nils Strindberg and Knut Frænkel launched the hydrogen balloon Örnen ('Eagle') from Svalbard to try to fly over the North Pole. The balloon came down on the pack ice after about two days, and the men trekked for months before dying on Kvitøya (White Island). Their camp, diaries and exposed film were found by chance by the Bratvaag expedition in 1930, and the photographs were developed, revealing images of the doomed journey.",
 [('Cause of death','Proposals include trichinosis from polar-bear meat, cold, vitamin A poisoning, carbon-monoxide poisoning and exhaustion; none is proven.'),
  ('Hero or reckless venture','The men were mourned as national heroes in Sweden; later historians stress the known leakiness of the balloon and Andrée\'s optimism.')],
 ['wiki:Andrée\'s_Arctic_balloon_expedition'],img='Andrée\'s_Arctic_balloon_expedition',end=1930),
X('zhokhov-island','Zhokhov Island Arctic hunters and sled dogs',H,'confirmed',3,-7000,['archaeological','genetic','radiocarbon'],
 "On Zhokhov Island in the New Siberian Islands, far above the Arctic Circle, archaeologists led by Vladimir Pitulko excavated a Mesolithic camp, about 9,000–8,000 years old, where people hunted polar bears and reindeer. The site preserved remains of dogs, sledge runners and tools. A 2020 genomic study showed that a roughly 9,500-year-old dog from Zhokhov belongs to an Arctic-adapted lineage ancestral to modern sled dogs, suggesting dog sledding is at least 9,500 years old.",
 [('Earliest sled dogs','Most researchers accept the genetic and archaeological case; some caution that sledge use is inferred from parts and dog morphology.'),
  ('A vanished land bridge','At the time sea levels were lower and Zhokhov was part of a larger landmass; the people are sometimes romanticised as a lost Arctic civilisation, but they were a mobile hunting group.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.aaz8599','wiki:Zhokhov_Island'],img=None,dateNote='c. 9,000–8,000 years ago (Wikipedia gives occupation from c. 6000 BCE)'),
X('royal-cemetery-ur','Royal Cemetery at Ur and Queen Puabi',H,'confirmed',1,-2600,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Leonard Woolley's excavations at Ur in southern Iraq (1922–34) uncovered about 1,800 graves, including some 16 'royal tombs' of around 2600–2500 BCE. Queen Puabi's tomb held an elaborate gold headdress, and the 'Great Death Pit' contained 74 attendants. Treasures included the Standard of Ur and the 'Ram in a Thicket'. CT scans of skulls published by the Penn Museum in 2009 suggest some attendants were killed by blows to the head.",
 [('Willing sacrifice or execution','Woolley pictured attendants drinking poison calmly; newer forensic work points to violent killing, though how widespread it was is debated.'),
  ('Woolley\'s flood','Woolley claimed a thick silt layer at Ur was evidence of Noah\'s Flood; geologists see a local river flood, not a universal one.')],
 ['wiki:Royal_Cemetery_at_Ur','wiki:Puabi'],img='Puabi',dateNote='c. 2600–2500 BCE'),
X('code-of-hammurabi','Code of Hammurabi',H,'confirmed',1,-1754,['epigraphic','textual'],
 "The Code of Hammurabi is a Babylonian legal text inscribed around 1755–1750 BCE on a basalt stele that shows King Hammurabi receiving authority from the sun-god Shamash. It contains about 282 rulings, including 'eye for an eye' penalties that varied by social class. The stele was taken to Susa as war booty by an Elamite king in the 12th century BCE and was found there by a French expedition in 1901; it is now in the Louvre.",
 [('Law code or royal showcase','Scholars debate whether it was applied in courts or was a model of royal justice; few surviving court records cite it.'),
  ('The first laws','It is often called the first law code, but earlier codes such as those of Ur-Nammu and Lipit-Ishtar are known.')],
 ['wiki:Code_of_Hammurabi'],img='Code_of_Hammurabi',dateNote='c. 1755–1750 BCE'),
X('kanesh-archives','Kanesh (Kültepe) merchant archives',H,'confirmed',2,-1950,['epigraphic','archaeological'],
 "At Kültepe (ancient Kanesh) in central Turkey, excavations have yielded over 23,000 cuneiform tablets from the houses of Old Assyrian merchants, dating to about 1950–1750 BCE. The letters, contracts and lawsuits record donkey caravans carrying tin and textiles from Assur to Anatolia in exchange for silver and gold, along with family affairs run by women in Assur. Personal names in the texts are the earliest evidence of the Hittite (Nesite) language.",
 [('An early market economy','Some economists see private firms, credit and partnerships; others stress the role of family and state institutions.'),
  ('Lost Anatolian homeland','The tablets inform debates about the origin of the Hittites and Indo-European languages in Anatolia, which remain unresolved.')],
 ['wiki:Kültepe'],img='Kültepe',dateNote='c. 1950–1750 BCE'),
]
